The '''Sarta Colony of Guajicutea (1609–1630)''' covers the first two decades of [[Sarta|Sartan]] colonial rule over [[Guajicutea]], from the establishment of the colony in 1609 through the stabilization of the colonial administration by 1630. The period was marked by [[War of the Mesogean|post-war diplomatic hostility]] from [[Burawa]] and [[Volisania]], two famines, rapid population growth through migration, and significant urbanization of the island's three primary cities.

The [[Treaty of 1610]] ended the [[War of the Mesogean]] between Burawa and Volisania, normalizing relations between the two powers in the {{w|New World}}. Both powers, however, resented Sarta's acquisition of Guajicutea and pursued non-military means of undermining the colony. A trade embargo forced Sarta to resupply the colony from [[Breisland|Breislandic]] [[Lurandia]], approximately 1,500 km away, with no intermediate ports. The embargo, combined with agricultural expansion and deforestation, contributed to famines in 1612 and 1617.

In 1617, the [[Petran Catholic|Sartan Petran Church]] negotiated with Volisania through the shared Petran Catholic tradition, securing Volisanian aid and a resumption of trade. The following year, the colonial government imposed a hard cap of 500 arrivals per year, as the island's population — swollen by Bayani migration, Sartan colonists, and natural growth — strained agricultural capacity. Despite the famines, the population reached approximately 60,000 by 1630, nearly double the pre-colonial peak.

The colony was administered by two governors during this period: [[Diego Vela]] (1609–1618), who oversaw the establishment of colonial institutions and weathered the trade embargo and first famine; and [[Alonso Mera]] (1618–1630), who managed the post-embargo stabilization, population growth, and urbanization of [[Tabakurí]], [[Waribá]], and [[Barikú]].

== Treaty of 1610 and trade embargo ==

The [[War of the Mesogean]] between Burawa and Volisania (1590–1610) was fought primarily in the {{w|Old World}}. In the {{w|New World}}, the conflict involved naval impressment and ship captures, but no land fighting on Guajicutea. The war ended with the [[Treaty of 1610]], which normalized relations between Burawa and Volisania.

Both powers, however, viewed Sarta's acquisition of Guajicutea as a strategic affront. Burawa had lost its colony without military defeat; Volisania had been outmaneuvered by Sarta's neutrality deal. Unable to retake the island by force without provoking a wider conflict, the two powers pursued economic pressure.

Beginning in 1610, Burawa and Volisania imposed a complete trade embargo on the Sartan colony. No Burawi or Volisanian goods were permitted to enter Guajicutea. Sarta, which had no other colonies or friendly ports in the {{w|Capuyaquiran Sea}}, was forced to resupply the colony entirely from [[Breisland|Breislandic]] [[Lurandia]], approximately 1,500 km distant. There were no intermediate ports of call along the route. Every shipment of food, tools, weapons, and personnel had to travel the full distance, making the colony a significant financial burden on the Sartan crown.

The embargo was not total in practice. Smuggling and informal trade continued at the margins, particularly through the [[Kingdom of Murani]], which retained its Burawi protectorate status and served as a conduit for limited exchange. But official trade was halted, and the colonial administration could not rely on consistent supply lines.

== Famines ==

The trade embargo and the colony's isolation produced two famines during the period.

The '''famine of 1612''' followed three years of embargo. The colonial administration had not yet established sufficient agricultural self-sufficiency, and the disruption of imported foodstuffs — previously a mainstay of the colonial economy under Burawi and Volisanian trade — left the population vulnerable. The famine was most severe in the southern agricultural districts, where plantation agriculture had been reorganized for cash crop production under the colonial administration and food crops were insufficient.

The '''famine of 1617''' was more severe. Five years of embargo had depleted colonial reserves, and deforestation-driven soil erosion on cleared slopes reduced agricultural yields. The famine prompted the [[Petran Catholic|Sartan Petran Church]] to intervene.

== Petran Church intervention (1617) ==

The Sartan Petran Church, which had replaced both the Burawi Catholic and Volisanian Petran Catholic hierarchies on Guajicutea in 1609, shared its religious tradition with Volisania's Petran Catholic Church. This shared tradition provided a diplomatic channel that political authorities could not or would not use.

In 1617, the Sartan Petran Church negotiated directly with Volisania through the Petran Catholic network. The church argued that the humanitarian situation on Guajicutea — two famines in five years — demanded relief regardless of political differences. Volisania, which had its own economic interests in resuming trade with the Capuyaquiran, agreed to provide aid and to resume limited trade with the Sartan colony.

The resumption of trade was not a full normalization of relations. Volisania did not recognize Sarta's sovereignty over Guajicutea, and Burawa did not participate in the arrangement. But the Petran Church intervention broke the embargo and established a practical trade relationship that alleviated the colony's supply crisis.

== Population and migration ==

Despite the famines, the colony's population grew rapidly during this period, driven by migration and natural increase.

The [[Bayani]] migration, which began in 1609, continued throughout the period. Bayani were a people persecuted in [[Balisca]], a Volisanian province, and the Sartan colony's laissez-faire labor policy offered them refuge and employment in agricultural fields. Bayani arrived through [[Waribá]] and settled primarily in the southern and western agricultural districts.

The Sartan colonial population also increased. Administrators, military personnel, missionaries, merchants, and their families arrived from Sarta and from Breislandic Lurandia. The colonial administration invested heavily in bureaucracy and military presence, as Guajicutea was Sarta's first and only colony and served as a strategic foothold in the Capuyaquiran.

By 1618, the colonial government imposed a hard cap of 500 arrivals per year. The island's population was growing faster than its agricultural capacity could support, particularly given the ongoing deforestation and soil erosion. The cap applied to all migrants, including Bayani, but did not include Sartan colonial personnel dispatched by the crown.

The population reached approximately 60,000 by 1630, nearly double the pre-colonial peak of approximately 40,000 (c. 1400 CE). The growth was driven by Bayani migration, Sartan colonization, and natural increase among the Guaji population, which had recovered from the [[Great Pestilence]] of the 1530s and the disruptions of the colonial transition.

== Deforestation and agriculture ==

The colonial administration pursued agricultural expansion to feed the growing population and to produce cash crops for export. This required clearing forested land, particularly on the volcanic slopes of the interior highlands and the southern lowlands.

Deforestation accelerated during the embargo years (1610–1617), as the colony could not import food and had to maximize domestic production. The clearing of slopes for agriculture led to soil erosion, particularly on the steeper volcanic terrain, reducing agricultural yields over time and contributing to the famine of 1617.

After the resumption of trade in 1617, the colonial administration continued to expand agriculture but with somewhat more attention to sustainability, as imported foodstuffs could supplement domestic production. The southern lowlands remained the center of plantation agriculture, while the central river valleys and western leeward lowlands were developed for food crops.

== Urbanization ==

The three primary cities of Guajicutea — [[Tabakurí]], [[Waribá]], and [[Barikú]] — underwent significant urbanization during this period, as the colonial administration invested in infrastructure and the population concentrated in urban centers.

=== Tabakurí ===

[[Tabakurí]], the inland capital, became the seat of the colonial governor and the administrative center of the colony. The colonial administration constructed government offices, a Sartan Petran Catholic cathedral, military barracks, and colonial residences. The city's population grew as Sartan administrators and their families settled there, and as Guaji and Bayani migrated to the capital for employment in the colonial bureaucracy and service economy.

=== Waribá ===

[[Waribá]], the northeastern port, served as the colony's primary port and naval base. Sartan ships arrived through Waribá, and the city's port facilities were expanded with new docks, warehouses, and naval installations. Waribá was also the primary point of entry for Bayani migrants and the center of the colony's trade with Volisania (after 1617) and Breislandic Lurandia. The city's population grew rapidly, driven by port labor, military personnel, and migrant communities.

=== Barikú ===

[[Barikú]], the western port, served as a secondary port and military outpost. The Volisanian fort constructed at Barikú under the post-1554 settlement was repurposed by the Sartan colonial administration. After the resumption of trade in 1617, Barikú's port activity increased, though it remained smaller than Waribá. The city's growth was driven by military presence and trade, particularly with the western Capuyaquiran islands.

== Governors ==

The colony was administered by two governors during this period.

=== Diego Vela (1609–1618) ===

[[Diego Vela]] was the first colonial governor of Guajicutea, appointed by the Sartan crown upon the establishment of the colony in 1609. Vela oversaw the initial consolidation of colonial authority: the replacement of the purged feudal bikabás with Sartan provincial administrators and the renaming of the sixteen feudal territories with Sartan provincial names — a mix of saint names and geographic descriptors following metropolitan naming conventions — the expulsion of the Burawi and Volisanian Catholic churches and their replacement with the Sartan Petran Catholic hierarchy, the implementation of the language policy requiring Sartan for religious and commercial activity, and the early Bayani migration.

Vela's tenure was dominated by the trade embargo and the famine of 1612. The colony's isolation and financial burden tested the colonial administration's capacity, and Vela struggled to establish agricultural self-sufficiency while maintaining the colonial bureaucracy and military. He was recalled to Sarta in 1618, likely in connection with the ongoing crisis and the transition to a new phase of colonial policy following the Petran Church intervention.

=== Alonso Mera (1618–1630) ===

[[Alonso Mera]] succeeded Vela as governor in 1618. Mera's tenure coincided with the post-embargo stabilization: the resumption of trade with Volisania (brokered by the Petran Church in 1617), the implementation of the arrival cap (1618), and the rapid population growth of the 1620s.

Mera oversaw the urbanization of the three cities and the expansion of colonial infrastructure. His administration managed the balance between agricultural expansion, population growth, and the colony's strategic role as Sarta's only colonial possession. By 1630, the colony had achieved a degree of stability: trade was flowing, the population was growing, and the colonial administration was established. The colony remained a financial burden on Sarta, but it was no longer in crisis.

